<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maxima Caesariensis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maxima Caesariensis was one of the provinces created in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> around 300. It covered south-eastern Britain, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>), which was also the seat of the vicarius of the Diocese of the Britains. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a> its governor held consular rank.</p></div>
